// Quantum kernel via SWAP test — the primitive behind QSVM. A classical
// SVM needs a kernel K(x, x') measuring how similar two points are; here
// each point is RY-encoded into a qubit, and the SWAP test estimates
// their overlap K(x, x') = |<phi(x)|phi(x')>|^2 directly on hardware.
// Real feature maps are deeper and multi-qubit (e.g. ZZFeatureMap) —
// this one's just a single RY so the circuit stays readable.

qubit x, xp, anc

RY 0.6 x   // data point A
RY 0.6 xp  // data point B (set equal to A for the "identical" test below)

H anc
CSWAP anc x xp
H anc

MEASURE

// The histogram shows every qubit, highest-index first, so with `qubit
// x, xp, anc` the leftmost bit is anc. Sum shot counts where that bit is
// 0 to get P(anc=0); kernel K = 2*P(anc=0) - 1.
//
// quell run qsvm_kernel.quell
// Identical points (x=x'=0.6): P(anc=0) ~100%, K~1.0.
// Orthogonal points (x=0, x'=PI): P(anc=0) ~50%, K~0.0.
